  "Question": "The solution of the equations \\[ \\begin{aligned} 2x-3y&=7,\\\\ 4x-6y&=20 \\end{aligned} \\] is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x=18,\\) and \\(y=12\\)",
    "B": "\\(x=0,\\) and \\(y=0\\)",
    "C": "There is no solution",
    "D": "There are an unlimited number of solutions",
    "E": "\\(x=8,\\) and \\(y=5\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The second equation's left side is exactly twice the first equation's left side. Therefore, any solution of the first equation must satisfy\n\\[\n4x-6y=2(7)=14.\n\\]\nBut the second equation requires the same expression to equal \\(20\\). Subtracting these requirements gives the impossible equality \\(0=6\\). Thus no pair \\((x,y)\\) satisfies both equations, so the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
